 - Config `codex_read_dirs` (#120): absolute directories (a pnpm store, `~/.cache/ms-playwright`) a codex job's prompt names as readable, an explicit read-only exception to «work only inside your own job directory». The codex command line does not change (the native sandbox does not confine reads; they are not added as writable roots, and under `worktree-full-access`, which has no sandbox, only the prompt keeps a job from writing there). `/`, paths overlapping the agent's state dir, paths in `/tmp` and paths that are, hold or lie in a secret dir (`~/.secrets`, `~/.ssh`, `~/.gnupg`, `~/.aws`, `~/.config/gh`, `~/.codex`, `~/.kube`, `~/.docker`, `~/.config/gcloud`, `~/.claude*`, the agent-jobs home; a minimum, not a complete list), also through links (resolved as far as the path exists, the missing tail appended; a path whose links cannot be resolved is refused) and case-insensitively on macOS, are refused. A hot key.
 - Config `prepare` (#120): per-repository commands (`repo_glob`, `dir`, `command`, `timeout_minutes`, default 10, max 60) the wrapper runs in a new job's worktree before its runner (claude or codex) starts, inside the job's containment, with network, without the guard or a sandbox, with the runner's environment (real `HOME`, so package managers find their stores and caches); output to `<jobdir>/prepare.log`. The first matching entry applies; only the first attempt of a job with a repository runs it, and a resume unless the newest earlier attempt that started a wrapper shows the worktree prepared (its step passed or its runner ran): a step that did not pass or no evidence (no wrapper record) runs it again, attempts refused at launch are skipped, and a job database read error refuses the launch. Its process group is recorded in the job row like the acceptance command's; leftovers that cannot be ended fail the job. A non-zero exit, a timeout or a missing `dir` fails the job before the runner starts («prepare failed (exit N after Ss); see prepare.log», also on the job-done line). `job_status` and `job_result` show `prepare` (command, dir, exit, `timed_out`, `duration_sec`), `agent-jobs status` a `prepare:` line. No `prepare` configured: no change. A hot key.
 
+### Security
+- The orchestrator's browser check no longer runs job-controlled code (#135). prompts/orchestrator.md and the skill told the orchestrator it may run `<job dir>/tools/ui-check.mjs` — a file in a directory the job can write, so a job (or content it read) could replace it and get code executed with the orchestrator's privileges; and scripts/ui-check.mjs resolved Playwright from the current directory's node_modules first, so a planted `node_modules/playwright` in a job worktree executed the same way. New command `agent-jobs ui-check <base-url> <path>... [--out dir]...`: it writes the script copy embedded in the binary to a private temp dir (removed afterwards) and runs that, forwards every flag and the exit code, and marks the run trusted (env `AGENT_JOBS_UI_CHECK=1`, forced, a stale value ignored). In trusted mode the script resolves Playwright only from `NODE_PATH`, the global npm root or a new explicit `--playwright <path>` (a package directory or entry file, used exactly as given), never from the current directory's node_modules; a job's own run of its `<job dir>/tools/ui-check.mjs` copy keeps the old resolution (its own privileges). The orchestrator docs now name only `agent-jobs ui-check`, never a job-dir or worktree copy. Tests: a planted `tools/ui-check.mjs` and a planted `node_modules/playwright` in the worktree the trusted run starts from are not executed (real node, fake `npm root -g`), the node script's static tests cover the trusted resolution and `--playwright`, and the orchestrator docs are checked for the banned pointers.
+
